{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"3062","artifact_type":"feature-context","name":"plan/feature-context-content-identity-control-set.md@sha256:8da68ef477dc795f480d5bd41b2c7bff4150203f963c060f9c2e1a7a097bc74a"},"owner_reference":"3062","content":"# ARTIFACT:DISCOVERY\n\n## Feature\n\nContent-identity-addressed control set for the markdown consumption engine (implements R8 of the agent-markdown-consumption contract), with `backlog_view` and `artifact_read` migrated onto it\n\n## Problem Statement\n\nPagination responses today carry only a page number and total \u2014 no content identity. Across stateless invocations, a caller cannot request \"the next page of what you just returned\"; every page request re-collects from the provider and re-parses. This is also a correctness gap: if source content changes between page requests, pages from two different versions can be silently served as though they were one document.\n\nAdditionally, `backlog_view` (the plugin's single most-called tool) currently runs its own separate, duplicate pagination logic and does not route through `progressive_markdown`'s engine at all \u2014 confirmed by impact-analyst (zero references to `ProgressiveMarkdownNavigator` in `backlog_core/server.py`). Building the control set into the engine alone would not back live `backlog_view` traffic. Repo owner decided (2026-08-20): #3062's scope expands to include migrating `backlog_view` onto `ProgressiveMarkdownNavigator`, and adding `artifact_read`'s R8 parameter/response-shape surface (the deeper \"route artifact_read through the engine at all\" work stays scoped to #3078, which tracks that `artifact_read()` currently bypasses the engine entirely).\n\n## Goals\n\n1. A response backed by the markdown engine's Navigation stage carries a stable content identifier (`content_id`), the command that produced it, and a generation timestamp, visible to the caller.\n2. A later page/navigate request is made using that `content_id` plus a page selector or ordinal address.\n3. Serving a later page from the control set never re-collects from the source and never redoes Generation \u2014 it cheaply reparses the already-stored raw content in memory.\n4. A request whose stored entry is marked stale is reported as such and auto-regenerates via the stored command, rather than being silently served.\n5. `backlog_view` is migrated off its own local pagination logic onto `ProgressiveMarkdownNavigator`, so the control set backs its live traffic on landing (not just new code nothing calls yet).\n6. `artifact_read` gains the same `content_id`/`hash`/`refresh` parameter and response-shape surface as `backlog_view`, matching R8, without necessarily being fully routed through the engine (that's #3078's deeper scope).\n\n## Anti-Goals\n\n1. NOT a durable reuse-optimization cache across unrelated tasks or sessions \u2014 it is temporary per-task navigation scratch space. Reuse across follow-up pages within one task is the design intent, not general caching.\n2. NOT session-scoped. No `session_id` anywhere in the design \u2014 this was proposed (ADR-3075-4) and explicitly reversed (ADR-3082-1). Also not per-tool, per-subcommand, or per-transport \u2014 one global store for every caller.\n3. NOT skipping writes on identity match \u2014 every full backend request always regenerates and always writes a fresh row.\n4. NOT an in-process cache (module-level dict, MCP server lifespan context) \u2014 the CLI is a fresh OS process per invocation with no memory of prior calls, so storage must be out-of-process regardless of transport.\n5. NOT unbounded storage \u2014 bounded by a soft global size target with LRU eviction, not an entry-count cap.\n6. NOT a full `artifact_read`-through-the-engine migration \u2014 that's #3078's scope; #3062 only adds the parameter/response-shape surface.\n\n## Requirements\n\n### Functional\n\n1. Storage: one SQLite database at `state_root()/control-set.db` (WAL mode), rows keyed by `content_id` alone. Follow `DispatchStateManager` (`backlog_core/dispatch_state.py`) as the closest in-repo precedent for manager shape (sync, standalone, `sqlite3.connect(check_same_thread=False)`, `ensure_schema()` via idempotent `executescript()`), but note it does NOT set `PRAGMA journal_mode=WAL` \u2014 that must be added fresh, along with `PRAGMA auto_vacuum=INCREMENTAL`. Async callers wrap calls in `asyncio.to_thread()`, matching the existing pattern.\n2. `content_id` is derived from the command (source, scope, parameters) plus the Generation stage's output for that command \u2014 not a hash of raw source alone, and not a hash of generated content alone (two different commands can coincidentally produce byte-identical generated output).\n3. Any write reachable from any caller that could invalidate a stored entry marks it `stale=true` (a marker column, never a row delete) \u2014 the stored command is preserved so a subsequent stale hit can auto-regenerate without the caller re-supplying the original scope.\n4. A caller may explicitly request revalidation or forced refresh via a `refresh` selector (`revalidate` | `force`). Both always write a fresh row; they differ only in what is reported \u2014 revalidation compares freshly computed identity to the previous one and reports whether it changed, forced refresh skips that comparison.\n5. Eviction: a periodic, rate-limited (hourly-to-daily, not per-write) sweep enforces a 40MB soft global size target via LRU-by-size trimming. `last_accessed_at` is bumped on read so an entry an agent is actively paging through is unlikely (best-effort, not guaranteed) to be evicted mid-navigation.\n6. A single entry larger than the 40MB target is let through as an explicit, documented exception rather than rejected.\n7. Concurrent-writer safety: the write sequence should be wrapped in an explicit SQLite transaction \u2014 full solution deliberately deferred to #3081 (tracked separately, not blocking this item's core scope).\n8. Invalidation entrypoint placement \u2014 CORRECTED per impact-analyst's confirmation: `task_status_hook.py` (`plugins/development-harness/skills/implementation-manager/scripts/task_status_hook.py`) is a subprocess-isolated `uv run --script` process (PEP 723, own interpreter) that never imports `dh_core`/`sam_schema` internals \u2014 all its writes route through `_call_sam_fastmcp()`, a `subprocess.run([..., \"fastmcp\", ...])` call into the SAM MCP server. It therefore CANNOT hold or call an in-process importable invalidation function directly. The invalidation trigger must live inside `sam_schema/server.py`'s `sam_task` write-action handling (the code the hook's subprocess call actually reaches), not as a separate entrypoint the hook itself imports. Same principle applies to `backlog_core/server.py`'s write paths (`backlog_add`, `backlog_update`, `backlog_groom`, `backlog_close`, `backlog_resolve`, `backlog_sync`, `backlog_strike_entry`, `backlog_update_sam_task_status`, `artifact_register`) and `sam_schema/server.py`'s `sam_plan`/`sam_task` non-read actions \u2014 invalidation logic belongs inside each of these existing handlers, called as a plain function that opens its own SQLite connection, marks matching rows stale, and closes it (consistent with ADR-3082-1's every-access-opens-its-own-connection model).\n9. `backlog_view` (`backlog_core/server.py`) is migrated from its own local pagination logic onto `ProgressiveMarkdownNavigator`. Gains new parameters (`content_id`/`hash`, `refresh`) and response-shape additions (content identity, generating command, generated-at) alongside its existing `selector`/`navigate`/pagination parameters, per R8.\n10. `artifact_read` (`backlog_core/server.py`) gains the same parameter/response-shape additions as `backlog_view` (content_id/hash, refresh, generating command, generated-at) \u2014 without necessarily being routed through the engine's Collection/Generation internals (that deeper migration is #3078's scope).\n11. CLI equivalents (the `sam_schema`-hosted CLI application and `dh_core`'s shared operations layer) need the same `hash`/`refresh`/`pagesize` parameter surface as the MCP tools \u2014 per `component-architecture.md`'s \"a capability added to one transport without the other is a gap.\" A task plan that wires only the MCP tools leaves R1 (one engine, all consumption) unsatisfied for the CLI transport.\n12. `docs/mcp-progressive-disclosure-contract.md` (documents today's shipped parameter set) needs updating as part of this same work, not as a follow-up \u2014 the doc change and the parameter change are the same piece of work.\n\n### Non-Functional\n\n1. Out-of-process persistence is required \u2014 the CLI has no long-running process memory to hold an in-process cache in.\n2. Global, not per-transport \u2014 MCP, CLI, and any other frontend caller resolve to the same entry for the same `content_id`.\n3. Disposable \u2014 the control set holds no authoritative data; every row is rebuildable from Collection + Generation on demand, so losing the whole database costs nothing but a cold cache.\n\n## Design Questions for Planning\n\nNone outstanding. Both scope-boundary questions raised during grooming (backlog_view migration scope, artifact_read parameter-surface scope) were decided by the repo owner \u2014 see Resolved Questions.\n\n## References\n\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/agent-markdown-consumption-contract.md` (R1-R8, especially R8)\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/adrs/ADR-3075-1-content-identity-and-cache-scope.md`\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/adrs/ADR-3075-2-stale-cache-recovery-and-write-invalidation.md`\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/adrs/ADR-3075-3-cache-metadata-visible-to-agent.md`\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/adrs/ADR-3075-4-out-of-process-session-keyed-control-set.md` (superseded in part by ADR-3082-1)\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/adrs/ADR-3082-1-sqlite-backed-bounded-eviction.md` (current authoritative design \u2014 read this one first)\n- `plugins/development-harness/CONTEXT.md` (Control set, Backend, Frontend glossary entries)\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/component-architecture.md` (component ownership map; states backlog_core's pagination logic \"duplicates this package's Navigator and Paginator and is scheduled for deletion, not maintenance\")\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/mcp-progressive-disclosure-contract.md` (documents today's shipped parameter set \u2014 needs updating as part of this work, see Functional Requirement 12)\n- `plugins/development-harness/progressive_markdown/navigator.py` (`ProgressiveMarkdownNavigator.load()` \u2014 the single chokepoint calling Collection then Generation; the control set's insertion point)\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/dispatch_state.py` (`DispatchStateManager` \u2014 closest in-repo SQLite manager precedent)\n- `plugins/development-harness/tests/test_dispatch_state.py` (direct test-pattern precedent)\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/implementation-manager/scripts/task_status_hook.py` (external write path \u2014 see Functional Requirement 8 for its actual subprocess-isolated calling shape)\n- #3081 (concurrent-writer transaction wrapping, deliberately deferred, tracked separately)\n- #3078 (deeper artifact_read engine-routing work \u2014 artifact_read currently bypasses the engine entirely, calling `provider.get_content()` directly)\n- #3091 (unrelated pre-existing `dh_paths.py`/`dispatch-state.db` slug-computation drift, filed separately, not part of this item's scope)\n\n## Resolved Questions\n\n| Question | Answer | Source |\n|----------|--------|--------|\n| Cache scope: session-lived or persisted? | Persisted, content-keyed, global \u2014 NOT session-scoped | ADR-3082-1 (supersedes ADR-3075-4) |\n| Does the identifier derive from raw markdown or the parsed tree? | Neither alone \u2014 derived from the command (source+scope+params) plus Generation's output | ADR-3075-1 |\n| What storage mechanism? | One global SQLite DB at `state_root()/control-set.db`, WAL mode | ADR-3082-1 |\n| What eviction policy? | Soft 40MB size target, LRU by size, periodic rate-limited (hourly-to-daily) cleanup | ADR-3082-1 |\n| Does every request always regenerate and write? | Yes \u2014 no skip-write-on-identity-match optimization anywhere | ADR-3075-3 |\n| How is staleness detected and handled? | Write-triggered marking only (`stale=true`, row preserved); a separate read-time hash-mismatch check was considered and rejected as redundant | ADR-3075-2 (as corrected this session) |\n| Is the stored content parsed or raw? | Raw content only \u2014 every read (including revalidation) reparses in memory; no parsed-tree cache | ADR-3082-1, ADR-3075-3 |\n| Does anything outside this engine need to trigger invalidation, and how? | Yes \u2014 task_status_hook.py's fastmcp subprocess call, and every mutating backlog_core/sam_schema server function. All must trigger invalidation from inside the existing in-process handler, not via a separately-imported function, since task_status_hook.py cannot import in-process state | Impact-analyst (corrects Wave 0 research's \"either/or\" framing) |\n| Does #3062 include migrating backlog_view onto the engine? | Yes \u2014 decided by repo owner | Grooming AskUserQuestion, 2026-08-20 |\n| Does #3062 include artifact_read's parameter surface? | Yes (the deeper engine-routing stays with #3078) \u2014 decided by repo owner | Grooming AskUserQuestion, 2026-08-20 |\n\n## Open Questions\n\nNone blocking.\n\n## User Confirmation\n\n- [x] Discovery content is drawn from ADRs merged this session (PR #3080) plus impact-analyst/technical-researcher verification against live source, plus two scope-boundary decisions made by the repo owner during grooming (2026-08-20). Ready for alignment-analyst and groomer.\n"}